<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Song</h1><p class="st-meta">1040 BCE – 286 BCE (approx.) · capital Shangqiu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The state where the descendants of the Shang kings kept their ancestral rites under Zhou rule, home of Zhuangzi and the scene of China's first disarmament conference.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the failed rebellion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weizi">Weizi Qi</a>, an elder brother of the last Shang king, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangqiu">Shangqiu</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, so that the Shang royal sacrifices would continue. Song was ranked as a duchy, the highest grade of Zhou state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Song preserved <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> customs, and other states mocked its people as slow-witted in stories like the farmer waiting by a tree stump. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiang-of-song">Duke Xiang of Song</a> sought the hegemony but lost to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hong">Battle of Hong</a> in 638 BCE after refusing to attack the enemy mid-crossing. In 546 BCE the Song minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-xu">Xiang Xu</a> hosted a peace conference of fourteen states. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> was a native of Song.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-yan-of-song">King Yan of Song</a>, who took the royal title, conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-teng">State of Teng</a> and fought his neighbours until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a>, with Wei and Chu, destroyed Song in 286 BCE and divided its lands. Song was later used as a dynastic name, most notably by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-song.jpg" alt="State of Song" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1040 BCE – 286 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shangqiu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Weizi Qi, Duke Xiang of Song, King Yan of Song</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Song (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-663&amp;focus=state-of-song" aria-label="State of Song on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7620 932 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Song lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7940 1188L7768 1193M7940 1188L7969 1105M7940 1188L7928 1163M7940 1188L7968 1165"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7969 1105h0M7928 1163h0M7968 1165h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7940 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7940 1188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-663&amp;focus=state-of-song">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

